F1.12 D4 fix review of A1.7 (b2bb7cb) — recovery test suite
================================================================

Fix summary (A1.7):
  scripts/test-recovery.sh adds Stage 3b (completed install, dual-config check
  of /limine.conf vs /EFI/BOOT/LIMINE.CONF, boots SeaBIOS+OVMF expecting
  NOVA_REL1_OK), Stage 4a USB EIO interruption, Stage 4b SPECS empty guard
  + SWEEP_TOTAL < 15 failure, release-aware --marker in boot_check/poll_for_marker,
  and creates the payload kernel by patching bytes
  kbytes[idx:idx+12] = b"NOVA_REL1_OK" over NOVA_BOOT_OK\n
  (11 bytes into a 12-byte slice → shrinks the binary by 1; verified it still boots).
  tests/test_recovery.py adds TestUsbRemovalRecovery EIO tests,
  TestCompletedInstallSync (incl. test_plant_a_missing_efi_twin_commit_detected,
  asserts rc 137 then root seq 1 vs efi seq 0), TestSweepStateCount
  (a local re-implementation of the guard, not the script's).

Findings F1–F4 (from docs/reviews/d4-freebuff.log):

F1 (blocking). The atomic swap can lose its entire second commit and D4 stays
  green. With img.patch_entry(efi_entry_off, ...) removed (plant A), the UEFI
  twin keeps pointing at the factory kernel while /limine.conf moves to the new
  release, yet every check passes and the harness prints PASS. No D4 scenario
  boots a completed install on either firmware, and no scenario compares the two
  config chains after an install. A UEFI machine would run an old kernel forever
  and nothing here would say so.

F2. The boot oracle is release-blind. Every boot is graded on the single string
  NOVA_BOOT_OK, and the payload kernel is the built kernel with a few marker
  bytes appended that are never executed, so the old kernel and the new kernel
  are indistinguishable on the serial line. Even a boot of the post-commit
  state cannot show that the installed release ran. Grading on a version or
  sequence string printed by the new kernel would fix F1 and F2 together.

F3. Scenario 2 (USB removed mid-install) is not a QEMU test and does not model
  USB removal. No EIO, no short read, no failed write: the "USB" cases call the
  same os._exit fault hook as power loss, and the four TestUsbRemovalRecovery
  cases never start QEMU. The claim in docs/team.txt that USB removal was shown
  "bootable under QEMU" rests on power-loss boots of the same states.

F4. A sweep that tests nothing reports success. When the state-list probe fails
  (section 5), the harness prints "0 states tested, 0 failures" and does not
  set FAILED. Nothing asserts the expected count of 15, so 15 boots can vanish
  silently. The harness should assert the expected count, as it already asserts
  exit codes.

